        </tr>
      </tbody>
    </table>
  </div>

  <blockquote class="evidence">Most ads are reviewed within 24 hours, although in some cases it may take longer, and any edit to the creative starts a new review.</blockquote>
  <p class="source">Source: Meta Business Help Center, "About ads in review," read Oct. 8, 2026. Platform help article, page undated.</p>

  <blockquote class="evidence">Most TikTok ads are reviewed within 24 hours; editing the creative triggers a review that may take up to 24 hours.</blockquote>
  <p class="source">Source: TikTok Ads Help Center, "Ad Review FAQs," updated February 2025, read Oct. 8, 2026.</p>

  <blockquote class="evidence">Most Google ads are reviewed within one business day, and an ad needed by a particular date should go in several days in advance.</blockquote>
  <p class="source">Source: Google Ads Help, "About the ad review process," read Oct. 8, 2026. Platform help article.</p>

  <p>"Most" is doing work in all three sentences. A rejected ad goes back to the designer, comes back as new creative and joins the queue again. That second trip is why the table leaves two days, not one.</p>

  <p>Google adds one condition worth a line in the plan: a new landing page has to be finished before the ad pointing at it can be reviewed. If the Black Friday page is in build, the ad waits with it.</p>

  <h2>Why does Black Friday paid social go live a week early?</h2>

  <p>Because a new ad starts in a learning period, and Meta says how long that period usually lasts.</p>

  <blockquote class="evidence">An ad set usually leaves the learning phase after about 50 results in the week after its last significant edit, and adding a new ad or changing the creative counts as a significant edit.</blockquote>
  <p class="source">Source: Meta Business Help Center, "About the learning phase" and "Significant edits and learning phase," read Oct. 8, 2026. Platform help articles, pages undated.</p>

  <p>During that week, Meta says, delivery is less stable and the cost per result is usually higher. Drop fresh creative into a running ad set on Black Friday morning and the ad set spends Black Friday relearning.</p>

  <p>So the calendar puts Black Friday ads in market on Thursday, Nov. 19, eight days out. The learning week closes on Thanksgiving, and the files have to be approved by Tuesday, Nov. 17, to clear review in time.</p>

  <p>Cyber Monday is the trap. A Cyber Monday ad added on Nov. 30 restarts learning on Cyber Monday itself. The cleaner route is to build it into the Black Friday batch, approve both together and schedule the switch. Meta reviews a scheduled ad when it's submitted, not when it starts running.</p>

  <h2>What does Amazon's calendar add?</h2>

  <p>An earlier deadline than anything on the social side.</p>

  <blockquote class="evidence">The deal submission window for Black Friday Week and Cyber Monday runs from July 8 to Oct. 20.</blockquote>
  <p class="source">Source: Amazon Seller Central news post, "Holiday 2026: Same fees, same eligibility, earlier deadlines," US store, July 2026. The marketplace's own announcement.</p>

  <p>That is 38 days before Black Friday. By Oct. 20 the deals themselves are in, products and deal prices included. The images and A+ pages around them are not, and they run on their own clock.</p>

  <blockquote class="evidence">A+ Content review takes up to seven business days.</blockquote>
  <p class="source">Source: Amazon Seller Central, A+ Content guide, US store, read Sept. 10, 2026. Marketplace help article, page undated.</p>

  <p>Counted back from the same Nov. 19 in-market date, a refreshed A+ page has to be submitted by Tuesday, Nov. 10. Add three days of sign-off and the files are due Thursday, Nov. 5. On the windows the ecommerce design service page publishes, roughly two weeks for a single-SKU pack and three to four weeks for a 20 to 50 SKU catalog, that puts the brief at Oct. 20 for one product and Oct. 6 for a catalog.</p>

  <p>Oct. 6 has passed. On those numbers, a catalog refresh that hasn't been briefed won't clear A+ review before Black Friday Week. Cut it to the hero products, or keep the current pages.</p>

  <p>Keeping them costs less than it sounds, because an A+ page can't carry the deal anyway.</p>

  <blockquote class="evidence">A+ Content may not include pricing or promotional language, time-sensitive references, or shipping details.</blockquote>
  <p class="source">Source: Amazon Seller Central, A+ Content guide, US store, read Sept. 10, 2026, rechecked Sept. 14, 2026. Marketplace help article, page undated.</p>

  <p>The Black Friday message lives in the deal itself and in the ads that point at the listing. The A+ refresh is about the product, which is why it can be briefed before the season's offers are final. Amazon hadn't published the 2026 start date of Black Friday Week when this page was reviewed. If it opens before Nov. 19, every Amazon date here moves earlier. The <a href="/solutions/platforms/amazon">Amazon platform page</a> covers the marketplace work in more detail.</p>

  <h2>What changes in December?</h2>

  <p>The deadline stops being a sale and becomes a delivery date.</p>

  <blockquote class="evidence">For expected delivery before Dec. 25 in the contiguous US, send by Dec. 17 for Ground Advantage and First-Class Mail, Dec. 18 for Priority Mail and Dec. 19 for Priority Mail Express.</blockquote>
  <p class="source">Source: US Postal Service newsroom release, Sept. 22, 2026. The carrier's own recommended dates.</p>

  <p>From Cyber Monday to that Ground Advantage date is 17 days. That is the whole gifting window that ships at standard rates, and it needs its own creative: gift guides, "order by" banners and email headers that name the date.</p>

  <p>Then the message flips. On Dec. 18 the "order by" banners have to come down and something else has to go up: express shipping, gift cards, store pickup. That switch is the Christmas row in the calendar, in market Friday, Dec. 18, with a brief due Monday, Nov. 30. That's Cyber Monday. Few retail teams have a spare hour that day, so send the December brief out with the Cyber Monday one.</p>

  <p>The December gifting row has the same problem from the other side. Its files need approval by Friday, Nov. 27, Black Friday itself. Approve them before Thanksgiving.</p>

  <p>Royal Mail's 2026 last posting dates weren't available at source when this page was reviewed, so UK "order by" dates aren't in the calendar. For Christmas Day itself, anything organic can be written in November and scheduled.</p>

  <h2>When do Boxing Day and the January sales start in Europe?</h2>

  <p>Boxing Day is Saturday, Dec. 26, in the UK. The bank holiday moves to Monday.</p>

  <blockquote class="evidence">In England, Wales and Scotland, Christmas Day falls on Friday, Dec. 25, 2026, and the Boxing Day bank holiday on Monday, Dec. 28, as a substitute day.</blockquote>
  <p class="source">Source: GOV.UK, UK bank holidays, read Oct. 8, 2026. Government data.</p>

  <p>A Boxing Day sale that opens Saturday morning has to clear review by Wednesday, Dec. 23, and its files land with approvers on Friday, Dec. 18, the last full working week before Christmas. That's the real deadline. The calendar counts Dec. 24 and Dec. 31 as working days; if your approvers are off, move those rows a day earlier.</p>

  <p>France regulates its sale dates, and the rule is fixed in advance.</p>

  <blockquote class="evidence">Winter sales open on the second Wednesday of January at 8 a.m., moved to the first Wednesday when the second falls after the 12th.</blockquote>
  <p class="source">Source: Service Public, the French government's information service, Dec. 12, 2025. The regulated rule, as published for the 2026 sales.</p>

  <p>In January 2027 the second Wednesday is the 13th, so the sales open Wednesday, Jan. 6, at 8 a.m. across most of metropolitan France. Four eastern departments set their own dates. The official 2027 notice hadn't been published when this page was reviewed; the date above is the rule applied to the calendar.</p>

  <p>Anywhere in the EU, a sale visual that announces a reduction carries one more dependency.</p>

  <blockquote class="evidence">Any announcement of a price reduction must show the prior price: the lowest price the trader applied in at least the 30 days before the reduction.</blockquote>
  <p class="source">Source: Directive (EU) 2019/2161, Article 6a of Directive 98/6/EC, applied from May 28, 2022. EU legislation; national rules vary in detail.</p>

  <p>For a Jan. 6 sale, those 30 days run through December's own promotions. The artwork can be finished early, but the price on it waits for whoever owns the price history. This describes production practice, not legal advice.</p>

