  <p>A shoot adds its window on top of the batch that uses its footage. If the Black Friday cuts need a new studio shoot, the brief moves back up to two weeks, to Friday, Oct. 16.</p>

  <h2>Where does AI production shorten the chain, and where doesn't it?</h2>

  <p>It shortens the making. It doesn't touch the queues.</p>

  <p>What generation compresses is versioning: one approved idea turned into each format and offer, in each market. A product photographed once can sit in a gifting scene in early December and a sale scene on Boxing Day without anyone going back on set. In the <a href="/app">hubStudio app</a>, a team edits from up to four source pictures, runs 1 to 10 images at a time at up to 4K, crops each result to the network formats in the Image editor and keeps the season's files together in a Campaign. Approvals sit on one thread in Validation with every version kept, so the Cyber Monday variant doesn't reopen the Black Friday conversation. Organic posts for Thanksgiving and Christmas Day can be written in November and scheduled to Instagram, Facebook, LinkedIn, X and TikTok (Beta).</p>

  <p>None of that moves anyone else's deadline. Meta, TikTok and Google review every ad. The learning week runs whatever made the creative. Amazon's deal window closes on Oct. 20, and A+ review can take up to seven business days. The USPS ground date is Dec. 17. The prior price on a European sale visual waits for the pricing team.</p>

  <p>A physical product is physical, too. A SKU nobody has photographed needs a shoot or a 3D model before anything can be generated from it. And Amazon's product image guide asks for a photorealistic, fully AI-generated person to be tagged as a synthetic performer in the image's metadata (one more line on the checklist, not a reason to avoid it).</p>

  <p>Our studio decides asset by asset what gets shot and what gets generated. The <a href="/services/design/ad-creative">ad creative service</a> covers the paid social side of that split, the <a href="/services/design/ecommerce">ecommerce design service</a> the marketplace side.</p>

  <h2>What about Singles' Day and China?</h2>

  <p>China runs its own season. Singles' Day on Nov. 11 and the 618 festival in June follow the Chinese platforms' published milestones, which open well before the day itself, and the <a href="/resources/insights/singles-day-618-production-calendar">11.11 and 618 production calendar</a> counts those back the same way this page does. A brand selling in both runs two calendars. In November they overlap.</p>

  <p>Reviewed Oct. 8, 2026, against every source named on this page.</p>

  <h2>Questions about the 2026 holiday calendar</h2>

